WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.960
- My field is high-energy
theoretical physics.

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.043
(overlapping speech)

00:00:17.353 --> 00:00:19.125
- [Narrator] The language
of science can be

00:00:19.125 --> 00:00:21.550
a bit difficult to understand.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:23.630
But the reason science
is hard to listen to

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:25.650
is because it's hard to communicate.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.615
Just ask Natalie, a climate scientist

00:00:27.615 --> 00:00:30.290
who teaches at George Mason University.

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:31.880
- Often with my class, I have

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.140
the students' attention
for only a few minutes,

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.877
so it becomes very
important for me to think,

00:00:36.877 --> 00:00:39.620
"How can I become a more
effective communicator?"

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.430
- [Narrator] That's where
actor Alan Alda comes in.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:45.180
He runs an organization that
helps scientists like Natalie

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.350
clearly communicate their field of study,

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.569
and they do it using improv comedy.

00:00:49.569 --> 00:00:51.894
- I wondered, if I worked with scientists

00:00:51.894 --> 00:00:56.600
on learning to do some of the
basic exercises of improv,

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.560
I could get this connection established

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.980
between scientists and their audience.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.613
So I thought, "Let me try it."

00:01:04.613 --> 00:01:06.217
- I'm Terry.

00:01:06.217 --> 00:01:08.380
(hums)

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.793
And this is Jen.

00:01:10.889 --> 00:01:12.800
(laughter)

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.440
- [Alan Alda] We established
a program to start

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.580
with basic improv exercises so that

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.680
scientists can communicate better.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.500
- I'm Melanie, and this is Sarah.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.830
- We train a really wide range of people.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:30.060
People from NASA, people
from the Parks Department,

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.080
physicians and medical researchers.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.322
We do a lot of physicalization of emotion

00:01:35.322 --> 00:01:38.650
and thoughts in these exercises.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:41.320
The main thing for me is that they

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.322
help you connect to another person.

00:01:44.322 --> 00:01:46.713
- Scientists, were you able
to explain your science?

00:01:46.713 --> 00:01:47.883
- Sort of.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:50.440
- [Narrator] Natalie took the workshop

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:51.770
to help teach her class.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:53.930
Since then, she's been able to connect

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:55.530
with her students in a new way.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.760
- The number one thing that
I've started doing differently

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.023
since the workshop is thinking,

00:02:00.023 --> 00:02:02.657
"Would I have known this word

00:02:02.657 --> 00:02:05.547
"when I was a first-year
university student?

00:02:05.547 --> 00:02:09.480
"Would I have been as
comfortable with this concept?"

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:13.122
And, as a result, that
makes me empathize more

00:02:13.122 --> 00:02:16.100
and clearly articulate the concepts

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.810
and the step-by-step process.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.040
So what would happen to this
molecule at zero kelvin?

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.570
So when I look out at my class
and I emphasize that message,

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:25.011
that everything they're
learning along the way

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:27.100
is so that they can have an understanding

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.020
of how climate change actually works,

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:30.326
and they can communicate that.

00:02:30.326 --> 00:02:32.909
(upbeat music)

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.360
(cheering and applause)

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:37.815
- [Narrator] Since they
began, the center has had

00:02:37.815 --> 00:02:41.126
over 14,000 people go
through the workshops.

00:02:41.126 --> 00:02:43.220
- I think good communication
is very important,

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.193
not just about science and
not just about medicine,

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:51.260
but good communication
makes the wheels turn.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:54.780
We need it in every activity of our lives.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.820
So your message is built

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.470
on the idea of connection and relating,

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:01.870
which I think is one of
the most important things

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.654
we can do for one another.

00:03:03.654 --> 00:03:06.404
(dramatic music)

